w-request\",\n      \"library\": \"com.microsoft.azure:msal4j-persistence-extension:1.3.0\",\n      \"summary\": \"No missing Maven dependency or Docker image is needed, but the library's real Linux code path (KeyRingAccessor via libsecret/D-Bus) can't run in headless CI without a live, unlocked Secret Service; the constructor calls verify() eagerly and will throw without one.\",\n      \"deterministic_setup\": [],\n      \"agent_guidance\": \"CI runs only ubuntu-latest (ci.json), so the Windows (DPAPI) and macOS (KeyChainAccessor) branches of PersistenceTokenCacheAccessAspect can never be exercised here — don't try to cover them. On Linux, PersistenceTokenCacheAccessAspect's constructor branches on PersistenceSettings.isOnLinuxUseUnprotectedFileAsCacheStorage(): if false (i.e. PersistenceSettings.Builder.setLinuxKeyring(...) was used), it eagerly builds a KeyRingAccessor and calls verify() synchronously in the constructor, which does a real write/read/delete round-trip against the D-Bus Secret Service through libsecret-1 (ISecurityLibrary statically calls Native.load(\\\"libsecret-1\\\", ...)). libsecret-1-0, dbus-daemon/dbus-run-session, and gnome-keyring-daemon are already present on the test/CI OS image, so the native library load itself will not fail — but no D-Bus session bus or unlocked keyring is running by default in a headless test process, so the verify() call will throw KeyRingAccessException and fail both the JVM and native-image test lanes. For a reliable primary test, build PersistenceSettings with setLinuxUseUnprotectedFileAsCacheStorage(true); this still exercises PersistenceTokenCacheAccessAspect's core read/write/lock logic and CacheFileAccessor/CrossProcessCacheFileLock via the normal generated scaffold with no extra setup. If coverage of the real KeyRingAccessor/libsecret/JNA path is wanted, the test's build.gradle would need a `test.doFirst` (or equivalent) that starts an ephemeral `dbus-daemon --session --fork --print-address` plus `gnome-keyring-daemon --unlock --components=secrets` (empty passphrase via stdin) and injects the resulting DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS/GNOME_KEYRING_CONTROL into the test JVM's environment before PersistenceTokenCacheAccessAspect is constructed with setLinuxKeyring(...) — this is inherently more fragile than the file-storage path, so treat it as best-effort/optional coverage rather than a hard requirement.\",\n      \"risks\": [\n        \"KeyRingAccessor.verify() runs inside the PersistenceTokenCacheAccessAspect constructor, so any test that reaches the real Linux keyring path will fail outright (not just skip a feature) unless a D-Bus session + unlocked Secret Service is set up first.\",\n        \"ISecurityLibrary.library is a static field that calls Native.load(\\\"libsecret-1\\\", ...) at class-init time, so merely referencing that class pays a native-library-load cost — currently satisfiable in this environment, but worth flagging as environment-dependent for native-image's JNA reflective-proxy generation.\",\n        \"Windows/macOS-specific code (KeyChainAccessor, DPAPI) is structurally unreachable in this repo's Linux-only CI matrix, so don't expect or chase coverage there.\"\n      ],\n      \"applied_setup\": [],\n      \"input_evidence\": [\n        {\n          \"name\": \"issue\",\n          \"summary\": \"#10068 Support for com.microsoft.azure:msal4j-persistence-extension:1.3.0; label=library-new-request; body_chars=39\"\n        },\n        {\n          \"name\": \"existing_tests\",\n          \"summary\": \"0 existing test file path(s) included\"\n        }\n      ],\n      \"model\": \"sonnet\",\n      \"input_tokens_used\": 46,\n      \"output_tokens_used\": 18741\n    },\n    \"metrics\": {\n      \"input_tokens_used\": 199972,\n      \"cached_input_tokens_used\": 1651968,\n      \"output_tokens_used\": 15039,\n      \"iterations\": 5,\n      \"cost_usd\": 2.2771,\n      \"generated_loc\": 151,\n      \"tested_library_loc\": 163,\n      \"metadata_entries\": 1,\n      \"test_only_metadata_entries\": 6,\n      \"code_coverage_percent\": 41.9\n    },\n    \"artifacts\": {\n      \"metadata_file\": \"metadata/com.microsoft.azure/msal4j-persistence-extension/1.3.0/reachability-metadata.json\"\n    }\n  }\n}\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1790141517552}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_z3GwJ0E2R6Zg13K3Mzi82lHb|fc_0aed6916d99a7bbe016ab3644d40d887d28a7c7c7e38bb191e", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"latest\" : true,\n    \"metadata-version\" : \"1.15.0\",\n    \"source-code-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/microsoft/azure/msal4j/$version$/msal4j-$version$-sources.jar\",\n    \"repository-url\" : \"https://github.com/AzureAD/microsoft-authentication-library-for-java\",\n    \"test-code-url\" : \"https://github.com/AzureAD/microsoft-authentication-library-for-java/tree/v$version$/msal4j-sdk/src/test/java\",\n    \"documentation-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/microsoft/azure/msal4j/$version$/msal4j-$version$-javadoc.jar\",\n    \"description\" : \"MSAL4J (Microsoft Authentication Library for Java) enables applications to acquire security tokens from the Microsoft identity platform to authenticate users and access secured web APIs. It supports Azure AD v2.0, Azure AD B2C, and various OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect flows including client credentials, authorization code, device code, and integrated Windows authentication.\",\n    \"tested-versions\" : [\n      \"1.15.0\"\n    ],\n    \"allowed-packages\" : [\n      \"com.microsoft.aad.msal4j\"\n    ]\n  }\n]\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1790141517552}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_bniCHPt1TUfRApiN1yzLY7nF|fc_0aed6916d99a7bbe016ab3644d40e087d2b1c8976351e22374", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "[\n  {\n    \"latest\" : true,\n    \"metadata-version\" : \"1.3.0\",\n    \"source-code-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/microsoft/azure/msal4j-persistence-extension/$version$/msal4j-persistence-extension-$version$-sources.jar\",\n    \"repository-url\" : \"https://github.com/AzureAD/microsoft-authentication-library-for-java\",\n    \"test-code-url\" : \"https://github.com/AzureAD/microsoft-authentication-library-for-java/tree/v1.15.1/msal4j-persistence-extension/src/test/java\",\n    \"documentation-url\" : \"https://repo1.maven.org/maven2/com/microsoft/azure/msal4j-persistence-extension/$version$/msal4j-persistence-extension-$version$-javadoc.jar\",\n    \"description\" : \"MSAL4J Persistence Extension implements the ITokenCacheAccessAspect interface from the Microsoft Authentication Library for Java (MSAL4J) so applications can persist their token cache to platform-specific secure storage. It encrypts the cache with DPAPI on Windows, stores it in Keychain on macOS, and uses the Secret Service API (via libsecret) on Linux, relying on JNA to call the native platform APIs.\",\n    \"tested-versions\" : [\n      \"1.3.0\"\n    ],\n    \"allowed-packages\" : [\n      \"com.microsoft.aad.msal4jextensions\"\n    ]\n  }\n]\n"}], "isError": false, "timestamp": 1790141517552}, {"role": "toolResult", "toolCallId": "call_6xs3xJCVc6k7EUEWopabDcM3|fc_0aed6916d99a7bbe016ab3644d40e487d28baa6aaf03fbd5c2", "toolName": "read", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "/*\n * Copyright and related rights waived via CC0\n *\n * You should have received a copy of the CC0 legalcode along with this\n * work. If not, see <http://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/>.\n */\nplugins {\n    id \"org.graalvm.internal.tck\"\n}\n\nString libraryVersion = tck.testedLibraryVersion.get()\n\ndependencies {\n    testImplementation \"com.microsoft.azure:msal4j:$libraryVersion\"\n    testImplementation 'org.assertj:assertj-core:3.22.0'\n}\n\ngraalvmNative {\n    binaries {\n        test {\n            buildArgs.add('--enable-url-protocols=http,https')\n        }\n    }\n    agent {\n        defaultMode = \"conditional\"\n        modes {\n            